1 NAME Ole Ingebrigtson /Birkedal/
2 GIVN Ole Ingebrigtson
2 SURN Birkedal
2 _MARNM Birch
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 17 AUG 1874
2 PLAC Dirdal, (Forsand)Gjesdal, Rogaland
2 ADDR
1 BAPM
2 DATE 13 SEP 1874
2 PLAC Forsand, Ryfylke, Rogaland
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1 MAR 1962
2 PLAC Hinckley, DeKalb, Illinois, United States
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} census 1875-https://www.digitalarkivet.no/census/person/pf01052935002056
2 CONT
2 CONT dåp-SAST, Høgsfjord sokneprestkontor, H/Ha/Haa/L0001: Ministerialbok nr. A 1, 1854-1875, s. 145 Brukslenke for sidevisning: https://www.digitalarkivet.no/kb20051209020173
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography Ole Ingebrigtson (Birkedal) Birch was born on August 7, 1874 in Dirdal, Rogaland, Norway. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole immigrated to the United States in 1893.
2 CONT
2 CONT On February 23, 1909, Ole became an United States Naturalized Citizen in the Circuit Court in DeKalb County, Sycamore, Illinois. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1910 Federal Census listed Ole as living in Shabbona Township, DeKalb, Illinois. His occupation was listed as farmer. On the farm next door lived his future parents-in-law Tjol (Fredriksen) and Ingeborg (Tobiasdotter) Fredrick
2 CONC son, his future brother-in-laws Theodore and Ingval Fredrickson and his future wife Inga Fredrickson. [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole Ingebrigtson (Birkedal) Birch married Inga Fredrickson on April 1, 1911 at Lee, DeKalb, Illinois.
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 12, 1918, Ole registered for the World War I Draft in Sycamore, DeKalb, Illinois. He was living with his wife Inga in Shabbona Township, DeKalb, Illinois. His date of birth was listed as August 7, 1874 and his occupat
2 CONC ion was listed as farming. His height was listed as medium, his build as medium, his eye color as blue and his hair color as brown. [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT In a March 17, 1919, newspaper Real Estate column, it was stated, "Shabbona - ...... Ole Birch warrant deeded to Andrew Berg, part sec 27 and 34, $24,615. [5]
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1920 Federal Census listed Ole as living with his wife Inga, his sons Elliot Olaf and John A., his daughters Bertha I. and Esther, his parents-in-law Tjol and Ingeborg Fredrickson and his brother-in-law Ingval Fredrickson in S
2 CONC habbona Township, DeKalb, Illinois. His occupation was listed as farmer. [6]
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1930 Federal Census listed Ole as living with his wife Inga, his sons Elliot Olaf, John A., Henry W. and Magnus Emery and his daughters Bertha I., Esther, Martha Elizabeth and Evelyn L. in Squaw Grove Township, DeKalb, Illinoi
2 CONC s. His occupation was listed as farmer. [7]
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1940 Federal Census listed Ole as living with his wife Inga, his sons Henry W. and Magnus Emery, his daughters Esther, Martha Elizabeth and Evelyn L. and his mother-in-law Ingeborg Fredrickson in Squaw Grove Township, DeKalb, 
2 CONC Illinois. His occupation was listed as farmer and he had completed the eighth grade. [8]
2 CONT
2 CONT In an October 8, 1941, newspaper article, it was stated, "One of the loveliest fall weddings was solemnized Sunday, Oct. 5, when Dorothy Westphall became the bride of Henry Birch at St. Paul's Evangelical Church, in Hinckley. Mrs.
2 CONC  Birch is the daughter of Mr. and Mrs. B. R. Westphall and Mr. Birch is the son of Mr. and Mrs. Ole Birch of Hinckley. .... The matron of honor was Martha Birch, a sister of the bridegroom. ...... The bridegroom's attendants were 
2 CONC John Birch, a brother, of the groom, who served as best man, Oliver Westphall, a brother of the bride, who served as groomsman. Ushers were Magnus Birch and Elliott Birch, brother of the bridegroom. ....... The bride and groom lef
2 CONC t on a short wedding trip and will reside on a farm southwest of Hinckley on their return." [9]
2 CONT
2 CONT In an April 6, 1945, newspaper column under "Hinckley", it was stated, ".... Peter barr has moved to the farm just west of Hinckley where Ole Birch lived for several years. Mr. Birch has gone to Elburn." [10]
2 CONT
2 CONT In an October 27, 1948, newspaper column under "Hinckley", it was stated, "Saturday afternoon, Evelyn (Lorraine) Birch, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Ole Birch of Elburn, became the bride of Wayne McCrea, son of Mr. and Mrs. Howard McC
2 CONC rea of Big Rock. The ceremony was held at the home of the bride's parents, .....Marjorie McCrea, sister of the groom was the maid if honor and .... Magnus Burch, brother of the bride, was the attendant of Mr. McCrea. ...... A thre
2 CONC e-tiered wedding cake was served, Martha Birch, sister of the bride serving the cake. At the punch bowl was Mrs. Jens Staurland sister of the bride. Mrs. Henry Birch at the coffee service and Esther Birch served the groom's cake. 
2 CONC Mrs. Walter Bock, sister of the groom had charge of the guest book. ..... Both the bride and groom are are graduates of the Hinckley High School and Mrs. McCrea is employed at the General Electric in DeKalb. Mr. McCrea, a veteran 
2 CONC of World War II is engaged in farming." [11]
2 CONT
2 CONT In a February 28, 1949, newspaper Obituary, it was stated, "Terry Yields to Leukemia; Rite Tuesday - Elburn, Fed. 28 - Terry Lee Birch, 2-year-old son of Mr. and Mrs. John Birch, died Saturday morning at his farm home near Elburn 
2 CONC of leukemia. ..... burial in Oak Ridge Cemetery, near Sandwich. ...... Less than a month ago, physicians discovered that he was a victim of leukemia. Terry had been well and husky until last month. Terry Lee Birch was born January
2 CONC  9, 1947, in Aurora. He is survived by his parents, one brother John Robert, 7 month old, and his grandparents, Mr. and Mrs. Ole Birch and Mr. and Mrs. John Lallemang of Sandwich." [12]
2 CONT
2 CONT In a June 4, 1949, newspaper column under "Hinckley", it was stated, "Mrs. Henry Birch was taken by ambulance on Tuesday to the St. Mary's Hospital in DeKalb where she is in an oxygen tent. her condition is reported as slightly im
2 CONC proved. Mr. and Mrs. Ole Birch are caring for their grandchildren while their mother is confined to the hospital." [13]
2 CONT
2 CONT In a November, 25, 1949, newspaper column under "Hinckley", it was stated, "Thanksgiving Day. At the home of Mr. and Mrs. Henry Birch were Mr. and Mrs. Ingval Frederickson and family and Mrs. Jens Staurland and family of DeKalb, M
2 CONC r. and Mrs. Elliott Birch and family of Clare, Mr. and Mrs. Wayne McCrea and son of Big Rock, Mr. and Mrs. Ole Birch and sons and Mr. and Mrs. John Birch and son of Elburn and Martha Birch, public health nurse of Clinton, Ill." [1
2 CONC 4]
2 CONT
2 CONT In a November, 25, 1950, newspaper article, it was stated, "Mr. and Mrs. Ole Birch and son, Mr. and Mrs. John Birch and son, Mr. and Mrs. Henry Birch and family and Mr. and Mrs. Wayne McCrea and son were guests Thanksgiving Day at
2 CONC  Malta in the home of Mr. and Mrs. Elliott Birch and family. The Jens Staurland family of Malta were also guests." [15]
2 CONT
2 CONT In an October 29, 1952 newspaper Birth Announcement, it was stated, "Mr. and Mrs. Elliott Birch of Clare are the parents of a daughter, Kathleen Ann, weighing seven pounds and one ounce, born this morning, Wednesday, Oct. 29, 1952
2 CONC , at the Glidden Memorial Hospital, DeKalb. Mrs. Carrie Keller of Pearl City, and Mr. and Mrs. Ole Birch of Elburn are the baby's grandparents and she has a brother also at home." [16]
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage ended when Ole's wife Inga passed away on June 3, 1960.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole passed away on March 1, 1962 in Hinckley, DeKalb, Illinois at age 88. He was buried in Johnson Grove Cemetery in Waterman, DeKalb, Illinois. [17]
